# Plan commit workflow

When creating or updating a plan, include a dedicated **Testing** section, a **Commit strategy** section, and a final **commit-await-approval** todo. Do not auto-commit when implementation finishes.

## Testing section (required)

Every plan must include at least one dedicated `## Testing` section (same class of requirement as Commit strategy). Cover:

- What will be tested and at which layer (unit / simulation functional / client) as applicable
- What is out of scope for automated tests
- A short fallout / regression run note after implementation

Doc-only or pure-config plans still need a short Testing section (e.g. “no automated tests; manual check …” or “link updates only”).

## Workspace git layout

This project is the **marloth-strategy** git repo (toplevel `/workspaces/marloth-strategy` when opened alone). A multi-root workspace may also include sibling repos (e.g. minimap); run baseline and commit git commands in **each affected repo**. Do not assume a single-repo workspace.

## 1. Baseline snapshot

In each git repo that may be affected, run read-only git commands and record results in a Commit strategy section:

- `git status --short`
- `git diff`
- `git diff --staged` (if anything is staged)
- `git log -3 --oneline`

## 2. Classify dirty files

Label each changed path:

- **Pre-existing** — dirty before this plan
- **Plan-scoped** — will change during implementation
- **Unrelated** — out of scope; exclude from plan commits

Include related pre-existing changes in the same per-repo commit. For unrelated dirty files, note "exclude" or ask before mixing.

**Standing include (marloth-strategy):** Dirty paths under `src/MarlothStrategy.Simulation/config/**` are **always Include** with the marloth-strategy plan commit unless the user explicitly excludes them.

## 3. Per-repo commit plan

Default: **one commit per affected repo** (not one squashed commit across repos).

For each affected repo specify:

- files to include
- files to exclude
- draft commit message (1–2 sentences, why not what; match recent `git log` style)

## 4. Final todo (always last)

Add a plan frontmatter / todo item `commit-await-approval` as the **last** item:

> **Commit (await user approval)** — After implementation and verification, re-run git status/diff per repo, reconcile with this strategy, commit only when the user explicitly requests it.

Keep it **pending** during planning and implementation unless the user asks to commit.

## 5. Commit strategy section template

Use this shape:

```markdown
## Commit strategy

### Baseline (plan created)

- marloth-strategy: clean | dirty — list pre-existing files

### Intended commits

#### marloth-strategy

- Include: …
- Exclude: …
- Draft message: "…"

### User trigger

Say **commit** (or **commit per the plan**) after review.
```

If additional git repos are affected, add a baseline bullet and an Intended commits subsection for each; keep one commit per repo.

## Commit execution

Only when the user explicitly says "commit" / "go ahead and commit" / "commit per the plan":

1. Re-snapshot git state in each affected repo.
2. Compare to the plan; if scope differs materially, pause and confirm.
3. Follow the Git Safety Protocol: status + diff + log in parallel, stage only planned files, HEREDOC message, verify after.
4. Repeat per affected repo. Never force-push or amend unless user rules allow.

Do **not** auto-commit when implementation finishes.
